                <pb facs="0644" n="610" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v15p1/pg610"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">320/11–1252: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Department of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">New
                                York</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-11-12T22:26:00-05:00">November 12, 1952—10:26
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Delga 183. Re Korea. USGADel advised by
                    Commonwealth sources of following account of most recent conversation between
                    Indian Ambassador in Peiping and Chou
                        En-lai: Chou En-lai is
                    reported to have said that release of PWs from
                    American control is the important step. Chou convinced that if all PWs
                    were released under some neutral control they would all “gradually” be
                    repatriated. It assumed this info conveyed to Indian delegation at UN and may
                    account in part for Menon’s present
                    instructions and his persistent determination to table some resolution
                    Friday.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Acheson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
